// U3 L3 Food Labels â€” 30 questions

::U3L3-01::All nutrient values on a Nutrition Facts label relate first to the {=serving size ~package color ~advertisement ~consumer's body size}

::U3L3-02::A serving size is {=standardized label information, not an automatic personal prescription ~the amount everyone must eat ~a health rating ~an advertisement}

::U3L3-03::If one serving has 250 Calories, two servings have {=500 ~250 ~750 ~125}

::U3L3-04::If one serving has 400 mg sodium, two servings have {=800 mg ~400 mg ~600 mg ~200 mg}

::U3L3-05::If one serving has 4 g fiber, 1.5 servings have {=6 g ~4.5 g ~8 g ~2 g}

::U3L3-06::The %DV shows {=how much a nutrient in one serving contributes to a reference Daily Value ~an exact personal prescription ~total product quality ~body size}

::U3L3-07::Under the general guide, 5% DV or less is {=low ~high ~a prescription ~unsafe}

::U3L3-08::Under the general guide, 20% DV or more is {=high ~low ~zero ~personalized}

::U3L3-09::A value of 12% DV is {=between the general low and high cutoffs ~low ~high ~a diagnosis}

::U3L3-10::Added sugars are {=included within total sugars ~added to total sugars again ~identical to protein ~not listed}

::U3L3-11::A label lists 14 g total sugars including 8 g added sugars. What is correct? {=Total sugars are 14 g, of which 8 g are added. ~Total sugars are 22 g. ~Added sugars are 14 g. ~Sugar cannot be interpreted.}

::U3L3-12::Ingredients are generally listed {=in descending order by weight ~alphabetically ~by color ~by healthfulness}

::U3L3-13::An unfamiliar ingredient name is {=not automatically evidence of danger ~always unsafe ~proof of poor quality ~a medical diagnosis}

::U3L3-14::When comparing two products, first check whether {=serving amounts are reasonably comparable ~package colors match ~prices are identical ~claims sound positive}

::U3L3-15::A product has fewer Calories. What can be concluded? {=It has fewer Calories per stated serving; broader quality needs more evidence. ~It is healthier for everyone. ~It contains no nutrients. ~Everyone should choose it.}

::U3L3-16::A package says â€œhigh protein.â€ The strongest next step is to {=check serving size, label amount, claim meaning, and context ~accept the claim as complete proof ~ignore the label ~judge by appearance}

::U3L3-17::A marketing claim differs from standardized label information because it is primarily {=persuasive language that must be checked against evidence ~a required nutrient amount ~a personal prescription ~always false}

::U3L3-18::A seller's financial interest means {=evidence should be examined carefully, not that every statement is automatically false ~the claim is always false ~the label is invalid ~no comparison is possible}

::U3L3-19::Which source feature strengthens credibility? {=Relevant expertise and transparent supporting evidence ~Follower count alone ~Product sales alone ~A testimonial alone}

::U3L3-20::Which question best checks purpose? {=Why was this information created and is something being sold? ~Is the package bright? ~Is the product popular? ~Is the name short?}

::U3L3-21::â€œEnergy boostâ€ should first be evaluated by asking {=what â€œenergyâ€ means in this claim ~whether the package is attractive ~whether the product is famous ~whether everyone needs it}

::U3L3-22::A claim is supported only when {=relevant evidence actually matches the claim ~it appears on the front package ~an influencer repeats it ~it uses scientific-sounding words}

::U3L3-23::Which is a limitation of a label? {=It does not by itself describe the entire dietary pattern or individual context. ~It contains serving information. ~It lists nutrients. ~It uses standardized units.}

::U3L3-24::Product A has 20% DV fiber and Product B 12% for comparable servings. What is supported? {=A is higher in fiber by this comparison. ~A is healthier for everyone. ~B is harmful. ~Fiber determines total quality.}

::U3L3-25::A fictional goal is less added sugar. Which evidence is most relevant? {=Added sugars per comparable serving ~Package color ~Follower count ~Total package weight alone}

::U3L3-26::A fictional goal is more calcium. Which comparison is most relevant? {=Calcium amount or %DV for comparable servings ~Calories alone ~Marketing slogans ~Ingredient-name familiarity}

::U3L3-27::Why is â€œhealthiest productâ€ a weak prompt? {=Appropriateness depends on a stated goal, overall pattern, and individual context. ~Labels contain no evidence. ~No products contain nutrients. ~Only advertisements matter.}

::U3L3-28::A testimonial is {=a reported personal experience, not automatically scientific evidence ~a standardized label ~a required Daily Value ~a serving calculation}

::U3L3-29::Which reasoning sequence is strongest? {=Claim â†’ serving check â†’ label evidence â†’ source credibility â†’ limited conclusion ~Claim â†’ popularity â†’ universal conclusion ~Color â†’ testimonial â†’ purchase ~One nutrient â†’ total health}

::U3L3-30::A product better fits a stated goal. This means {=the evidence supports that specific goal, not universal superiority ~it is best for everyone ~the other product is bad ~all consumers need it}

